8. Root Cause Analysis

Primary contributing factors:

Exposed RDP without MFA

Compromised administrator credentials

Lack of network segmentation

Insufficient privileged account protections

Internal RDP unrestricted

This was not a vulnerability exploit — it was credential abuse.

9. Containment & Remediation

Enable MFA for all privileged and remote accounts

Remove external RDP exposure (require VPN / Zero Trust)

Reset and rotate all privileged credentials

Reimage affected systems

Implement network segmentation

Monitor RDP activity and anomalous authentication patterns

Enforce credential hygiene and password policies

10. Impact Assessment

Domain controller compromised

Lateral movement confirmed

Malware executed

Ransomware staged

Encryption prevented by endpoint protection

Without Defender intervention, this incident likely would have resulted in full ransomware impact.

11. Lessons Learned

Valid credentials eliminate need for exploit-based access

RDP exposure remains a high-risk attack vector

MFA is critical for privileged accounts

Human-operated ransomware follows predictable lateral movement patterns

Early detection of RDP anomalies can prevent catastrophic impact

Analyst Reflection

This case highlights the importance of correlating authentication logs, endpoint telemetry, and process injection alerts to identify structured ransomware behavior before encryption occurs. It reinforces that credential security and remote access hardening are foundational defenses against enterprise compromise.

